#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "114232b4-2761-5665-9296-c185bd484017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275631} {:answer/id #uuid "7880da2d-e957-5ca8-8e13-bcb627935744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276569} {:answer/id #uuid "8c2c725f-185f-5f35-871e-79e343f1ed7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276749} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c9fbc8-b14c-5cbc-8b85-1785d478d974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276972} {:answer/id #uuid "f330e754-04e9-557c-9d3e-6d2ab7556a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f330e754-04e9-557c-9d3e-6d2ab7556a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277609} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8880f9f-c13c-59f9-8dce-1a13792353a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "68256c62-7faf-5747-9e8c-b2c67a0a95a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-161444" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 4\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n\nII. $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$\n\nIII. $∂A⊂A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/62/62c410c21e79e1bc688c1ff1a3440a207c62bcad42525bdc93fa2770b76d48bd.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/33/33a4738b642443c2535d347aa53ee2b7b80c800142c4844d94578769118ebfbc.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points, so we must have $∂A⊂A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b3101aa-ee9e-5940-b170-2f4e78802150" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Divisibility of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aea82579-6011-5d78-815f-f8aabe5c4c68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197943} {:answer/id #uuid "bc39ae1c-88fb-59c0-9d44-13ef1d661cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197944} {:answer/id #uuid "d784b2c9-3ed2-578f-a801-9d465553f6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197945} {:answer/id #uuid "06483019-fec0-52f6-9a75-e64b84b77d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197946} {:answer/id #uuid "791b39e6-b54c-5276-8c55-f6bb8fdf4bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d784b2c9-3ed2-578f-a801-9d465553f6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197945} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f973ddc-35f8-5608-8069-a0955b9994f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6ff943b-6246-50f5-830d-737c87bafa4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-92248" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $15\\,|\\,32b$ then $15\\,|\\,b$\n\nII. If ${7}^{2}\\,|\\,7b$ then ${7}^{2}\\,|\\,b$\n\nIII. If ${3}^{2}\\,|\\,25b$ then $9\\,|\\,b$" :question/worked-solution "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$c\\,|\\,ab\\,$ and $\\,\\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $\\,c\\,|\\,b.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(15,32)=1.$ Therefore, we have $15\\,|\\,32b$ and $\\text{gcd}(15,32)=1,$ which implies $15\\,|\\,b.$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, let $b=7.$ Then, ${7}^{2}∣7\\cdot 7$ but ${7}^{2}∤7.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}({3}^{2},25)=1.$ Therefore, we have ${3}^{2}∣25b$ and $\\text{gcd}({3}^{2},25)=1,$ which implies ${3}^{2}\\,|\\,b.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204623 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203900 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d05194c-249d-54fb-91f1-f614c0f5e584" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordered Pairs Belonging to a Non-Standard Relation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24ec285a-89b6-5e80-85cf-547592c0be93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411627} {:answer/id #uuid "29192154-5fca-5c4c-ba05-c1677c12f786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411628} {:answer/id #uuid "4a6224af-2752-5783-be20-5ad5862f62d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411637} {:answer/id #uuid "516f8b78-7d59-5ec9-ba7c-04b73f06ad86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411639} {:answer/id #uuid "85e2f0d3-5246-5ae6-9a99-5800a2b254f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24ec285a-89b6-5e80-85cf-547592c0be93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411627} :answer-field/id #uuid "856a64a3-53df-597c-9cc5-75ee45f124c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e6ad6a4-a09f-5fba-92c0-ff271a2f98be" :question/math-academy-id "q-80860" :question/problem "Given the relation $R$ on $Z$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x∣(y-1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $4\\,R\\,13$\n\nII. $3\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,11$\n\nIII. $8\\,R\\,3$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∣(y-1).$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. In this case, we have $4∣(13-1),$ which is true.\n\n- Statement II is true. In this case, we have $3∤(11-1),$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, we have $8∣(3-1),$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229633 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229158 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bdd2ceb-c14a-5a3d-bf03-692a1475fa41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02c467ea-d283-5e56-8a87-1b18aa02c984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302472} {:answer/id #uuid "45ccedb0-e55b-599b-8637-db4470585ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302898} {:answer/id #uuid "7cdec9ab-46e6-5596-8ec2-44c59b6c6b63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303232} {:answer/id #uuid "804dc170-f17c-5982-87c0-36daec01e968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303259} {:answer/id #uuid "dc1c1b2b-78f8-5fab-8359-ff09e51e76aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "804dc170-f17c-5982-87c0-36daec01e968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303259} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c636557-3959-5f68-a368-7e6b6fa87d4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97216a46-237d-5c6e-afbc-0108a663f4c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-332530" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =5,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\n- The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}=(1-5Δx)^{n}{y}_{n}.$\n- The method is stable for $|1-5Δx|<1.$\n- The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$\n- The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.25.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-5{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-5{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-5Δx){y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-5Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-5Δx)((1-5Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & =(1-5Δx)^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & =(1-5Δx)^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =(1-5Δx)^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-5Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-5Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-5Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-5Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <5Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.25$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092329 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19a03588-a6f2-5251-be03-4bdaa4eb280e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327155} {:answer/id #uuid "423f75d6-0783-5897-97b0-99de5db0c8d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327515} {:answer/id #uuid "759de309-55be-5420-9616-13433e8c09ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327943} {:answer/id #uuid "a248b3a7-3413-5a74-a48b-1e99f9ab94c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328339} {:answer/id #uuid "e38e145c-3810-5bad-b273-78afcc7cdd6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e38e145c-3810-5bad-b273-78afcc7cdd6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328928} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9e8f46b-385c-5762-a3ae-f76405c171c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c6cc47f7-3bf2-54bf-a0ea-9009aac01bb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-47447" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $7,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P+Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$\n- $P-Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $4$\n- $P\\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, suppose $P(x)={x}^{3}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{7}.$ Then $P+Q={x}^{3}+{x}^{7},$ which is not a polynomial of degree $10.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, suppose $P(x)={x}^{3}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{7}.$ Then $P-Q={x}^{3}-{x}^{7},$ which is not a polynomial of degree $4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{3}$ and ${x}^{7},$ respectively. Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P\\cdot Q$ must contain ${x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{7}={x}^{3+7}={x}^{10},$ meaning that $P\\cdot Q$ has degree $10.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a423260-bbbe-5ae5-9f27-db48fce5a7d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236382} {:answer/id #uuid "5b27cad1-42d8-5307-8cc2-5cc48adae5ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236384} {:answer/id #uuid "9dce0a55-5641-528c-9f93-21ec3cd96888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236804} {:answer/id #uuid "fa19be47-2169-5673-84c3-412cf1b7a226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237400} {:answer/id #uuid "feda186f-19bf-5fb8-9907-6309e3735091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b27cad1-42d8-5307-8cc2-5cc48adae5ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236384} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8ff4376-b626-5cd5-b078-07242fef4809" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0efb43bb-72c2-5796-b21b-537b0708d8ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-101995" :question/problem "Given that a function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$, has a removable discontinuity at $x=1$, and is differentiable at $x=-1$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\nI. It is differentiable at $x=0$\n\nII. It  is **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n\nIII. It is continuous at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false. Continuity does not imply differentiability. For example, the function $y=|x|$ is continuous but it's not differentiable at $x=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true.  A function that is discontinuous at a point can't be differentiable at this point (because differentiability implies continuity).\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, differentiability implies continuity."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237868 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91e0fa99-107e-554a-bca7-bad853c6cd4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236742} {:answer/id #uuid "9243ba2b-d92c-523d-8153-b97e690974ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236746} {:answer/id #uuid "c051bc6b-f385-5b06-a71c-62218d812193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237041} {:answer/id #uuid "ca847587-1091-5d6b-8cf0-c5d23b115e59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237103} {:answer/id #uuid "ecfb2f83-347a-5d8a-8e26-10289635d4e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecfb2f83-347a-5d8a-8e26-10289635d4e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237310} :answer-field/id #uuid "53dbf63f-8b12-5a21-b442-56f7cd97d4ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5280401d-3d75-5547-be89-ad4f6f6c9526" :question/math-academy-id "q-101997" :question/problem "Given that a function $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-5$, is differentiable at $x=-2$, and has a  vertical tangent at $x=0$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\n- It is **not** differentiable at $x=-5$\n- It  is continuous at $x=-2$\n- It is differentiable at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "Differentiability implies continuity. Hence,\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-5$, it can't be differentiable there either.\n\n- Statement II is true.   Since $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x=-2$ it is also  continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since we have a vertical tangent at $x=0$, the function $f(x)$ is not differentiable at this point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115381 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Functions are Differentiable at a Given Point From the Function Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14bcc2d5-8ff0-5de6-b9b7-8c70c668b43e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235942} {:answer/id #uuid "1af6eee1-8b75-5cbd-abfe-28d48340133b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235983} {:answer/id #uuid "5b8299b8-14e4-5661-916f-3e7a91907a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236388} {:answer/id #uuid "758d1cc1-c63d-566d-9f78-5af3c023e6cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236554} {:answer/id #uuid "82c9af49-99d8-5695-9534-6ef4ffbf18bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1af6eee1-8b75-5cbd-abfe-28d48340133b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235983} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8a8469b-04e3-5429-9f82-f67f5e5c1400" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6fe09a9-5d63-559e-a71c-67bb17b36b63" :question/math-academy-id "q-102163" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x-2, & x<0 \\\\ -2, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\n- It is continuous at $x=0$\n- It is differentiable at $x=0$\n- It is differentiable on the interval $(0,2)$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is true  Indeed, let's investigate the continuity of $f(x).$ It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, at  $x=0.$ At this point, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(2x-2)=2(0)-2=-2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}-2=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=f(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$ .\n\n- Statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0$ because it has a corner point there: \n\n$\\,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=2\\,$  if  $\\,x<0$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0\\,$ if $\\,x>0$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $f(x)$ is differentiable everywhere except at $x=0.$   Since $x=0$  doesn't belong to $(0,2)$, the given function is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176051 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e2a8cf2-64db-5b2f-b0f7-0c727cf492a9" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37bcf381-7f8a-5baa-9290-980479025998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236172} {:answer/id #uuid "607dfedf-36ef-54be-84e1-a22d3370bf39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236432} {:answer/id #uuid "6b296e58-25bf-5073-a97d-db4dc955015a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236492} {:answer/id #uuid "7bfca28b-3c9f-56f3-b358-5f4c8bd08815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236592} {:answer/id #uuid "ae5c2399-4da8-5d37-a130-7c781b78ea87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37bcf381-7f8a-5baa-9290-980479025998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236172} :answer-field/id #uuid "19c031a1-a523-51cd-984f-8f8228525268" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50bb01da-0246-5da5-b527-fc7a59519d36" :question/math-academy-id "q-103685" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=sec⁡x?$\n\n- The range of the function is $y=(-\\infty ,-1]$\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is an output of the function\n- $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is not a zero of the function" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](images/d0/d0f3e760be991c936baf5e9825cd582f086a73a8330032d24fd35fd5594b4864.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).$ Therefore, $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is not an output of the function.\n\n- Statement III is true since the function has no zeros."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176055 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a18aadb-3e8a-54d7-a5d7-e78208e77675" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "219b49f6-4024-5608-b300-3db553fd48e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236033} {:answer/id #uuid "5373b6dd-8882-5b2f-91b1-c818e364404a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236346} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b29b66-322b-5608-93ec-631968a3e9c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237175} {:answer/id #uuid "d7aeade8-d178-56b1-8a96-ed6d56fccb5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237182} {:answer/id #uuid "e7e40cd5-c7e0-586a-ae46-5c9379c1dfb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5373b6dd-8882-5b2f-91b1-c818e364404a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236346} :answer-field/id #uuid "b12e6f2a-4a8a-5fc1-91fb-89ef78acdfcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e25815e7-4d17-5965-b1f7-fc15c12bd9d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-103836" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x?$ Assume $n$ is an integer.\n\nI. The function has vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pi +\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.$\n\nII. The domain of the function is $[-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nIII. The period of the function is $\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=cot⁡x.$\n\n![](images/77/7785ad73f6e5212772c3053780ffd055f37cfb39927b6ddc3e40cd3a55b8ba4e.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The vertical asymptotes of the function are integer multiples of $\\pi $, i.e., $x=n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is false. The domain of the function is all real numbers except the values where the asymptotes occur, i.e., $x=n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement III is true. The period of $y=cot⁡x$ is $\\pi .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25229703-4cfb-5010-ae5c-f6a54503ced3" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity of Three-Piece Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18317632-9a46-5b18-bafd-820209526524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238261} {:answer/id #uuid "2b206fef-99a4-534a-b2b0-b6ae959fef99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238436} {:answer/id #uuid "6e34f467-9d96-5d96-adbd-58a85537abb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239005} {:answer/id #uuid "74c42b8b-18d3-5fba-a1e7-4a175cd7070f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239062} {:answer/id #uuid "75d8569c-8b78-5fc7-977e-d7799bd0a0bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75d8569c-8b78-5fc7-977e-d7799bd0a0bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239073} :answer-field/id #uuid "21361fe3-3c71-57a8-ba47-cf7a876c9edd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "23c6c497-7b53-5892-b312-12905adbf824" :question/math-academy-id "q-104384" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3},\\,\\, & x<2 \\\\ 8,\\,\\, & x=2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2},\\, & x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $f(x)$ at $x=2.$\n\nFirst, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=2$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={2}^{3} \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & =f(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=2$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =2(2)^{2} \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & =f(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=2,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240897 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129086 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9110c50a-62db-5447-b4d0-60819c80eb31" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties Related to Point Discontinuities Given a Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c362354-b121-53da-b1d0-32db9976b524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238164} {:answer/id #uuid "8d031b0b-6f60-5c10-a903-198316653435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239280} {:answer/id #uuid "a1745f6f-463b-5bbd-8815-814b9176348f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239460} {:answer/id #uuid "c6da6cc7-debd-54ae-a437-073b08d05ea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239806} {:answer/id #uuid "e002f9b5-f859-53ed-bd2b-7e968ac2c54e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c362354-b121-53da-b1d0-32db9976b524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238164} :answer-field/id #uuid "2228e72c-dac2-5e7f-aab5-a7eee153aa98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b8ec7f0-866e-5db4-b2de-0c9d1fe0bd7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-104572" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x+11}, & x<5 \\\\ 12, & x=5 \\\\ {e}^{5/x}, & x>5\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements is true?\n\n- $f(5)$ exists\n- ${lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}f(x)$\n- $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=5$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $f(5)=12$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true.\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}\\sqrt{x+11}=4, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}{e}^{5/x}=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that statement II is false and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}f(x).\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSince the second condition failed, $x=5$ is not a removable discontinuity. So, statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28bee080-f834-5a65-875e-64ff7347b686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238415} {:answer/id #uuid "5996a243-56ab-51f3-ad76-62c2e3bde6cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238824} {:answer/id #uuid "7e0ffc73-d2c1-5009-b10d-4151c9d3cd50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239152} {:answer/id #uuid "9a7e7d63-45c4-50a5-aaa2-0d3597c02017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239400} {:answer/id #uuid "9b6fe7c0-652f-5c4b-aba5-09bac5d9f309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b6fe7c0-652f-5c4b-aba5-09bac5d9f309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239410} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac3ebdbc-2590-5082-b160-ffed9036c31a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bcc06ba1-1065-5981-a107-14dc46cea80e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104984" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(2{x}^{2}-1) & 2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\ 2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} & {e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(2{y}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(0,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(0,0)>0$\n- $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)>0$\n- The point $(0,0)$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=(-1)^{2}-{0}^{2}=1>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=-1<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(0,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)<0,$ the point $(0,0)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2384084c-0ad8-5474-a426-2c2d4b61f394" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238367} {:answer/id #uuid "63e0a616-9b9c-5f18-b88e-79e60e3cf07f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238930} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd744ae-5143-52bc-be05-7bd8710afef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239417} {:answer/id #uuid "c346f462-1b1e-504d-9e90-c52eccd0c1c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239777} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e2cde0-867a-5cd6-807e-b33239302266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63e0a616-9b9c-5f18-b88e-79e60e3cf07f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238930} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e6c05c4-cb31-5b46-a21a-67880b6b5964" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e273e9fd-fb16-5b0e-b15b-fcbb70918d78" :question/math-academy-id "q-104995" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3y & 3{y}^{2}-3x\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(1,1)>0$\n- $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)<0$\n- The point $(1,1)$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3y & 3{y}^{2}-3x\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}-3y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}-3x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-3y)=6x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-3x)=6y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-3x)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6x & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=det({f}^{″}(1,1))={6}^{2}-(-3)^{2}=27>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=6>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(1,1)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)>0,$ the point $(1,1)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21f26a3f-f8aa-51d7-83bc-84d4d1f3e29c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238350} {:answer/id #uuid "9aec57c3-1ff8-5ede-b25f-4126b769414d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239404} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b955c8-064e-5c1a-9233-310b065bfe30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239754} {:answer/id #uuid "ce693687-08cd-5026-93b5-f84a347f03c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239863} {:answer/id #uuid "e60eec68-d021-5965-822a-8deec198de79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21f26a3f-f8aa-51d7-83bc-84d4d1f3e29c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238350} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d236ad3-8465-5855-af2b-83ac755c82b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0ffa6ad-f9fd-59d8-a6a4-bd61246d63d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-105064" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={n\\in N∣n-2\\ge 4}$ and $B={n\\in Z∣n+2\\ge 5}?$\n\n- $A⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $∅⊆A$\n- $B⊇A$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $n-2\\ge 4,$ that is to say, $n\\ge 6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA={6,7,8,9,10,11,…}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n+2\\ge 5,$ That is to say, $n\\ge 3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB={3,4,5,6,7,8,…}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is true. Every element in the set $A$ is an element of the set $B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is false. For example, $3\\in B$ but $3\\notin A.$\n\n- $∅⊆A$ is true. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\n- $B⊇A$ is equivalent to $A⊆B,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240976 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4050aada-7658-541f-be9b-3f3781195813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238610} {:answer/id #uuid "645d28d3-fe8f-52fc-842b-4f5ec36cb5fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238935} {:answer/id #uuid "79bab5cd-3fc0-5a56-a4aa-07edf3fa9de1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239104} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b76ccb-502a-51ef-89b3-83e8999e2f40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239787} {:answer/id #uuid "cc9bae77-3654-5d37-a473-0b619ae82373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4050aada-7658-541f-be9b-3f3781195813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238610} :answer-field/id #uuid "52ecb4b5-78e4-53b4-9866-6cccc44f6d8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e6954872-228a-577f-9a57-37ed3b0eaeb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-105065" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in Z∣x\\le 5}$ and $B={x\\in Z∣-7\\le x<3}?$\n\n- $A⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $B⊈A$\n- $A⊇B$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the integer numbers that satisfy the inequality $x\\le 5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA={…,-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $-7\\le x<3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB={-7,-6,-5,-4,-3,-2,-1,0,1,2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false.  For example, $5\\in A$ but $5\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true, while $B⊈A$ is false. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $A⊇B$ is equivalent to $B⊆A,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only\"."}]}
